    51	---
    52	
    53	Brom argued the middle, from the room's one good chair, in the register of weight, and he argued it in three sentences because three were what he had.
    54	
    55	"Prepare both roads." He turned his cup. "Karis files. The counsel argues. We win the sessions, because we do. And every night, after the filing, somebody packs one box." He looked at Lira. "You've checked the road east. Check it properly. Waystations, the Line, what's past it. Not to walk it. To know it." He looked at Cael. "You've never once gone into a fight without knowing the exits. This is a fight. Same law." And to the room: "Walk neither yet. Both roads open. That's all I've got."
    56	
    57	"That's a lot," said Lira.
    58	
    59	"It's three sentences," said Brom, and the room let him have it, because it was, and because it was also the first time any of them had said the word *box* aloud, and the word stayed in the room after he said it.
    60	
    61	Lira picked it up, after a moment, the way she picked up anything left on a floor.
    62	
    63	"What's in yours?"
    64	
    65	Brom considered it seriously, which was the only way he considered anything.
    66	
    67	"Clothes. The Copper certificate, because it's the only paper my father will accept as an answer to a question he asked when I was fifteen. My mother's letter." He turned the cup back the other way. "And the strapping. Two years of it, all of it, because you can buy strapping anywhere and I don't want to be somewhere I have to."
    68	
    69	"That's not much for a Velmere," said Lira, carefully.
    70	
    71	"No," Brom agreed. "It's the second box that's the problem."
    72	
    73	Nobody asked what the second box was. Cael charted the not-asking, and charted the way Brom let the not-asking stand, and wrote later that the second box was the review — the advancement review, docketed for term's end, the thing his family's ledger had demanded since he was a child and which he had never once mentioned in the same conversation as the word *east* — and that Brom had put it on the table by refusing to, which was the whole of the man's grammar.
    74	
    75	---
    76	
    77	Cael argued the first pole, and he argued it at full strength, because it was the strongest position he had ever held and he had three years of receipts for it and the receipts were real.
    78	
    79	"The hearing at Greyvane. The demonstration provision. Four semester stamps, renewed, on a Compact-observed record. An inspection that declined to find. A tournament their own clause put me in, and a match their own instrument couldn't score, and the instrument's keeper writing *no number goes here* rather than a lie." He said them in order, the way he wrote inventories, because the order was the argument. "Every time the people failed, the text held. Not because text is kind — because text is *there.* You can read it. You can find the orphaned clause. You can put a true record on a counter and make a registrar decline to falsify it. The people are weather. The text is ground."
    80	
    81	"Say the rest of it," said Karis. "You've got a fifth one you're not saying."
    82	
    83	"Denvash," Cael said. "A man in an office decided what I was in eleven seconds and there was no text in the room, and I was fourteen, and it took me three years and four institutions to get a sentence written down that undid it. Every good thing in my life since has been text somebody wrote down and then had to honor." He looked at Lira, whose question it was. "The system has never beaten me when the text was in the room. I'm not leaving the room while the text still works."
    84	
    85	"It's working now?" Lira said. "The seal said *lawful* and *proceeding* in the same paragraph."
    86	
    87	"The seal said lawful. On the record. Forever." He heard the sentence come out with more weight than he had meant to put on it, and let it stand, because it was true and the room was for true things. "The corridor's real. I've walked corridors. Every one of them had a door somebody hadn't read the text on. Karis found one in a compilation, one in a tournament charter, one in a squad rule. She'll find one in the code." He looked at the face-down papers. "I don't know which one. I know there's never once not been one."
    88	
    89	"And if the code doesn't have one," said Brom.
    90	
    91	"Then it's the first document in three years that doesn't."
    92	
    93	"That's the argument," said Karis. "I want to say plainly what kind of argument it is, because you'd want me to. It's an induction. Five instances, all in the same direction, no counter-instance yet — which is a strong finding and is also exactly the shape of every finding that has ever been overturned by the sixth instance." She said it without heat. "I'm not saying you're wrong. I've built my whole career on the same induction and I'd file it tomorrow. I'm saying the strength of it is the number of times it's held, and there is no number of times that makes a thing a law."
    94	
    95	Nobody answered that for a moment. It was, the log would concede that night, the best argument in the room, and it was made by the person the room existed to protect, and the room could hear the receipts in it, and the room could also hear the thing underneath the receipts, which none of them had a word for yet, and which Lira — who had been in a corridor at fourteen with a correct form and no word — came closest to naming.
    96	
    97	"You said the people are weather and the text is ground." She said it carefully. "I've been standing on ground I was sure of. It was a form with my name on it. Correctly filled, correctly stamped, correctly filed by a woman who wished me well. The ground was correct and it opened anyway." She looked at him. "That's not an argument against text. I know what the text has done for me; I've got a supersession because of it. It's an argument about what text is. It's not ground. It's a floor. And floors are held up by something, and I stopped being able to see what, about twelve days ago." She sat back. "I'm not saying leave. I'm saying: check the ground. That's all. Brom's three sentences. Both roads."
    98	
    99	"Both roads," Cael agreed, because he could agree to that without conceding anything, and the room heard him not concede, and let him.
   100	
   101	Karis wrote *both roads* in the notebook with a date. Under it she wrote a second line, which Cael read upside down and did not comment on: *road east — L. has waystations. Nobody has asked what we do about H.*
   102	
   103	The council ended as councils that had not decided anything ended, which was with tea.
   104	
   105	Brom made it, because Brom made it, and the making took twenty minutes and nobody left during them, which was the actual finding of the evening. Lira put her feet on the bench opposite and worked her landing-leg hip with two thumbs, on the coach's schedule, out of habit; the meet was three weeks out and the program did not care what the seal had ruled. Karis reread her own second line and did not amend it. Cael took the lamp down to three-eighths because three-quarters had been for reading and there was nothing left to read.
   106	
   107	"One thing on the record before we go up," Karis said, to the room, in the finding voice. "We didn't decide anything tonight. I want that stated, because in four months somebody is going to remember this evening as the night we decided, and it wasn't, and if we're going to be an institution about this we may as well be an accurate one."
   108	
   109	"It was the night we asked," said Lira.
   110	
   111	"That's what I'll write."
   112	
   113	"Write both," said Brom, from behind the pot.
   114	
   115	She wrote both. Then she gathered the face-down papers without turning them over, and the five of them went up through a residence that had been asleep for an hour, and the small room's lamp went out, and the one line of light at the shutter's bad joint went out with it, and the stores range and the court were dark from the assessment office's window by the time the man who had sat in the bench's middle got there.
   116	
   117	
   118	---
   119	
   120	The silence was Seln's, and the room let it be load-bearing.
   121	
   122	He had attended the whole council from the bench's middle, in the configuration the year had built, and he had contributed one thing, which was a procedural correction — when Karis cited the *fled* default, he had said, without opening his eyes, "The default enters at the fourth session's close, not the ruling's. Forty days from a flight, not from an adjournment. The code's precise about it," and Karis had corrected the notebook, and he had said nothing else. Not on the corridor. Not on the road east. Not on the boxes, or the text, or the ground. He had sat at the table he had spent a year and a half earning a seat at and had held the silence of a man who had a document in the room with him that nobody else could see.
   123	
   124	Cael charted the silence, because charting was what he had instead of asking, and because with Seln, asking had never once been the instrument that worked.
   125	
   126	Four registers of not-answering, he had counted at the last council. Tonight there were five, and the fifth was different in kind. Karis's silences were the pause before a citation. Brom's were weight. Lira's were the half-second in which she decided how much of a true thing a room could carry. Cael's own, he knew perfectly well, were inventory.
   127	
   128	Seln's, tonight, was none of those. It had duration and it had *direction*: he had not looked at the drill-book, or the papers, or the lamp. He had looked, twice, at Lira, when she said the road east. He had looked once, and for a long time, at Cael, when Cael said *I'm not leaving the room while the text still works* — and the look had a quality Cael could not chart and did not have a word for, and he tried three words in the log that night and struck all three, and left the entry as it stood:
   129	
   130	*Something in the room tonight cost him. Not the argument — he doesn't care who wins an argument. Something in it was a bill. He came, he sat, he corrected Karis on forty days, and he left the way he always leaves, which is before you notice he's standing. Enter it with a question mark, the way I enter everything he starts and doesn't finish. Fourth entry this term with a question mark on it. That's a pattern and I don't have the second half of it.*
   131	
   132	---
   133	
   134	Seln's own account of the council was kept where he kept everything, and it was longer than he had permitted himself in years.
   135	
   136	He went back to the assessment office after the tea. The office was dark; the counter's lamps had been out for three hours; the man had a key and a desk and fifteen years of reasons to be at both after hours. He did not light the counter lamps. He lit the desk lamp, which threw eight feet and no further, and sat down with the cross-index of the instrument in front of him — forty pages in his own hand, the best document about the petition that existed anywhere including at Ostrand — and did not read it, because he had written it and knew what it said.
   137	
   138	The office at that hour was the shape his working life had always had. A counter with nobody at it. A window with a court beyond it and, across the court, the residence's dark bulk — and in it the small room's shuttered window, which for three hours that evening had let out one thin line of lamp at the shutter's bad joint, and which was dark now, and which he had noticed in his first week and had never mentioned to anyone, because mentioning it would have meant explaining why he had looked.
   139	
   140	Fifteen years of product. Nineteen postings.
   141	
   142	He had done the arithmetic of the postings once, in his twelfth year, and had not enjoyed it. Nineteen towns. Four academies, two guild halls, a district seat, three provincial registries, and the rest of it the kind of place that had no name in the reports and got a designation instead. Four hundred and ten reports filed. Sixty-three subjects. Of the sixty-three, he could name fifty-eight; of the fifty-eight, he knew what had happened to nine, and had stopped, deliberately, at nine, because the tenth had been a woman in a river district and he had gone looking and had found out, and the finding out had cost him a year of sleeping properly and had taught him the only professional lesson he had ever learned twice.
   143	
   144	And a locked case on a shelf in his quarters that had outlasted every one of the nineteen. It had traveled to Norhold and back unopened. It had ridden in a cart under the same canvas as three boxes of a researcher's compiled truth without either archive knowing the other's contents, and he had sat on the cart's bench for two days beside those boxes and had thought about the symmetry for most of the second day.
   145	
   146	Two and a half years of this boy. A file that had begun as an assignment — *subject, enrollee, Halcenvane, unclassified, monitor and report* — and had become, by increments he had never permitted himself to date, the only file of his career he had wanted to be wrong about.
   147	
   148	The increments were on record even if the dating was not. The boy at the office counter in his first week, reading a form all the way through, both sides, and then asking a question about the seventh line that no enrollee in Seln's experience had ever asked because no enrollee had ever read the seventh line. The night at the records office with the lamp and the sightlines, a boy who had been handed a piece of another man's trade and had understood, without being told twice, that the debt was not the tradecraft but the exposure. A place at a table that had appeared under him one autumn without anyone announcing it, and which he had taken, and which he had never once acknowledged, and which he had come back to for a year and a half.
   149	
   150	And the sentence he had never once let himself finish, forming anyway, at the dark desk, in the audit-voice that was the only voice he had ever fully trusted:
   151	
   152	*The file's question was always the wrong question.*
   153	
   154	Whether the enrollee was a classification error. Whether the anomaly could be evaluated. Whether the provision could hold him, whether the tournament could score him, whether the seat could find him lawful. Every question the machine had ever asked about the boy was a question about whether the *boy* fit — and he had known, for a decade, sitting in rooms exactly like the trading floor at Ostrand, reading files exactly like the one on the respondent's table, that the question had never been about who fit. It had been about who *decided*, and by what instrument, and since when.
   155	
   156	He had the maintenance records. He had read them in a posting twelve years ago, by one candle, in a room he had been in lawfully and for another purpose entirely, and had duplicated them because duplicating was his habit and had been since his second year, and had gone on with the posting, and had not understood what he had duplicated for the better part of a year. The understanding had arrived in a corridor, unprompted, between two ordinary appointments, the way the worst understandings always arrived. And he had walked away from the job in the only way his trade allowed a man to walk away from it, which was by staying in it and keeping.
   157	
   158	*And I am the only person alive holding the file that proves whose question it was.*
   159	
   160	He did not open the case. He did not go and look at it. He noted, in the audit-voice, exactly what the case staying closed cost, because the cost had been abstract for fifteen years and had stopped being abstract at a table three hours ago.
   161	
   162	Every session the boy fought inside the instrument believing the instrument was merely broken. Every filing the researcher perfected against a corridor she had drawn without knowing who had built it. Every road the Fenmark girl checked with three carters at a ferry landing, learning the geography of a border her own government had never explained to her. Every box the big one did not yet pack. Every night the honest men filed true reports into a mill that did not care where the river wanted to go — Bracken's completeness, Withrow's charter, Ilsev's scope entered in the minute in her own words, Havel's not-looking, four hundred and ten reports of his own — all of it conducted by people who thought they were arguing with a malfunction.
   163	
   164	The audit had a column for that. It had always had the column. What it had not had, until twelve days ago, was a figure in it that a man could not carry.
   165	
   166	He sat with the last line for a long time, in the dark office, with the cross-index under his hands and the case on its shelf across the campus and a dark window four buildings away where a line of lamp had shown for three hours, and the line was the closest thing to a decision the audit-voice had ever recorded, and it was not one yet, and he knew the difference exactly.
   167	
   168	*He thinks he's arguing with a malfunction. I've read the maintenance records. Fifteen years I've been deciding to die with that sentence. The deciding has stopped being quiet.*

