     1	# Chapter 11 — The Escort Order
     2	
     3	They came to the gate on a grey Fifth-day morning at the second bell, four of them, on good horses, in traveling clothes with no livery, and the gate lodge's man — who had held that lodge for eleven years and had processed everything the bluff road had ever sent up — took one look at the paper the lead rider presented and sent his boy running for the registrar at a pace the lodge had never once required.
     4	
     5	The lodge had a procedure for everything and the procedure was posted inside the lodge door on a board the lodge-man had lettered himself. Deliveries: the arrivals book, the lane, the stores counter. Visitors on academy business: the arrivals book, the intake clerk, a chair. Registry instruments: the arrivals book, the registrar, and a note of the hour. Nothing on the board covered four unliveried practitioners on good horses with a sealed instrument at the second bell of a working Fifth-day, and the lodge-man — who was sixty-four and had a bad knee and had never in eleven years run anywhere — read the paper twice at his counter, put it down flat, and said to his boy, in an ordinary voice, "Bracken. Now. Run, don't walk, and don't stop to say why."
     6	
     7	Cael was on the covered walk when the boy passed, and he was in the gallery above the gate before Bracken reached it, because a runner from the lodge was a document about him and four years had taught him the arithmetic.
     8	
     9	The walk ran the yard's north side under a tiled roof on stone posts, and it turned above the gate arch in a short covered gallery with a waist-high parapet — six feet of stone and a view — where the intake liked to watch arrivals and where a student who stood still was furniture. He took the parapet's east end, which gave him the whole yard and put the morning's flat grey light behind him, and did not lean on it, and did not move again for twelve minutes.
    10	
    11	The yard below was Halcenvane's front room and Cael had catalogued it in his first month: forty feet by sixty, cobbled, sloping four inches toward the drain at the stable lane's mouth. The gate arch at the south. The lodge at the arch's east cheek with its door and its one window. The records hall steps at the north-east, six of them, wide. The covered walk's stair at the north-west, narrow, turning once. The stable lane running out at the west corner, which was the only exit from the yard that did not pass a door. Four ways out, five if a person counted the arch, and everyone counted the arch.
    12	
    13	He watched the yard fill in the way yards fill: the four riders dismounted, standing with their horses in the loose professional arrangement of people who had stood in yards before; the lodge-man at his door, not in it, which was a distinction he had chosen; two third-years crossing from the refectory who slowed, and looked, and were looked at by one of the riders, and went on at their own pace with their shoulders wrong. Bracken crossing the yard at a registrar's fastest dignified pace, which was a pace Cael had seen exactly twice. And behind Bracken, already summoned, already coming, Withrow, with the counsel beside her, because the counsel had not gone home after the fourth session and the not-going-home had been its own reading of the term.
    14	
    15	The whole of it took four minutes from the boy's run to the chancellor standing in her own gate, and Cael entered that number that night, because four minutes was fast and fast was expensive and the riders had presumably known what it would cost the academy to be that fast and had come anyway.
    16	
    17	---
    18	
    19	The paper was an escort order.
    20	
    21	Cael learned its contents in the yard's own acoustics, because Withrow required the lead rider to read it aloud.
    22	
    23	"The academy's practice is that instruments served at this gate are read at this gate," she said. She said it standing four feet inside her own arch with her hands at her sides. "You may read it, or my registrar may read it, and I would prefer you. Instruments sound different in the mouths of the people who brought them."
    24	
    25	The rider read it, in a level professional voice, without objection, and Cael took it down in the gallery on the back of a floor schedule with a pencil he had been carrying since Norhold for exactly this kind of morning:
    26	
    27	*To the Chancellor of the chartered academy at Halcenvane, and to the enrollee of record therein named. The registry, in support of the proceedings presently convened at the seat at Ostrand, directs the attendance of the said enrollee at the registry facility at the district seat for pre-evaluation assessment, transport and escort provided, effective on presentation. The academy is directed to produce the enrollee to the bearers of this order. Given under the Compact's hand.*
    28	
    29	Eighty-one words. Cael counted them twice on the schedule's back and then sat with the last five, which were the five every binding instrument the registry had ever issued closed with, and which he had read on four semester stamps and a tournament entry and a hearing's finding and had never once, until a grey morning in his own gate, heard read aloud by a man on a horse.
    30	
    31	"Presented under what seal?" said Bracken.
    32	
    33	The rider showed it.
    34	
    35	And Cael, from the gallery, watched thirty years of registrar read a seal and fail to place it.
    36	
    37	Bracken did it the way he did everything, which was completely. He held the paper at his reading distance. He turned it to the light — the flat grey off the roofs, which was the worst light in the yard and the only one available — and he moved twice to get the impression's shadow to lie the way he wanted it. He set his thumb beside the ring and read the ring's outer legend, and then the inner, and then the field. He looked at the stock's edge against the light, and at the back of the impression where the die had pushed the fibre through. He took, in all, a minute and forty seconds, in a yard where four practitioners and a chancellor stood watching him do it, and nobody said a word for any of it, because thirty years of a man's counter earns him a minute and forty seconds.
    38	
    39	Then he said, in the counter voice, the flattest one, "This is genuine registry stock. The seal is a registry-format seal. I do not recognize the issuing office, and I have processed instruments from every issuing office in this district and the three adjacent for thirty years."
    40	
    41	"The office is a facilities office," said the rider, without inflection. "Newly constituted for the proceedings' support."
    42	
    43	"Then it will have a constitution instrument on file at the district seat," said Bracken, "and the academy will verify it there before anything else happens."
    44	
    45	"The order is effective on presentation."
    46	
    47	"The order is effective on presentation *by an issuing authority*," said Bracken. "That is not a technicality and I would ask you not to receive it as one. A seal is not a signature. A seal is a claim that an office exists and that the office stands behind the paper. I can read a claim. I cannot verify one from a yard." He held the order out, flat, and did not let go of it. "I have never in thirty years refused an instrument I could place. I would be glad to place this one."
    48	
    49	---
    50	
    51	Withrow's refusal took four sentences, and she built it the way Karis built filings, each course bearing the next.
    52	
    53	The proceedings' schedule was the schedule; the seat had set it; no pre-evaluation assessment appeared anywhere in it. No facility transfer was contemplated by any instrument the academy had been served. The order's issuing authority was unidentifiable to the registrar of a chartered institution, which was itself a procedural defect requiring verification before compliance. And the enrollee was an enrollee in good standing of a respondent institution in active proceedings, whose production the code governed precisely, and the code's word was the only word that moved him.
    54	
    55	"The academy declines the order pending verification," she finished. "You may wait while we verify, or you may return with the constitution instrument. The gate will log either."
    56	
    57	The lead rider took that without any change of face at all, and then he offered the morning's one piece of pressure, and he offered it in the mildest form available, which was the form that told Cael more than anything else in the yard.
    58	
    59	"The academy's position is noted," he said. "The order runs to the enrollee as well as the institution. If the enrollee elects to attend voluntarily, the academy's refusal is not engaged, and the matter closes here."
    60	
    61	Cael, in the gallery, six feet above the arch, was not addressed, was not looked at, and was not — he checked, and checked again — visible to the man speaking, who had not once turned his head toward the covered walk. And the offer went up into the gallery anyway, precisely aimed, and hung there.
    62	
    63	He did not move. That was all it required of him and it was harder than it should have been, and he noted the hardness with interest: twelve minutes of standing still had cost him nothing, and four seconds of standing still while a stranger in his own gate said *voluntarily* cost him something he could feel in his hands.
    64	
    65	Withrow did not look up at the gallery either.
    66	
    67	"The enrollee elects nothing this morning," she said. "He is not in this yard, he has not been served, and I am not going to send for him so that he can be asked in front of you. If your office wishes to put a proposition to an enrollee of this academy, it may put it in writing, to me, and I will carry it to him myself, and he will answer it with his counsel beside him. That is what the word *institution* means and it is the only reason to have one."
    68	
    69	"Understood, Chancellor," said the rider.
    70	
    71	The four accepted the refusal without argument.
    72	
    73	That was the morning's loudest fact, and everyone in the yard understood it at once, and understood that they had been meant to: no protest, no citation of authority, no second reading of the order, no request that the refusal be entered in any form, no demand for a written statement of grounds — which Bracken said afterward was the piece that should frighten people who understood paper, because a genuine issuing office refused at a gate would have demanded a written refusal in nine seconds, and would have wanted it more than it wanted the enrollee, since a written refusal is the thing you build the next instrument on.
    74	
    75	They did not want the next instrument. They had come for something else and had got it.
    76	
    77	The lead rider took the paper back, inclined his head the correct depth to a chancellor, and the four of them remounted and rode back down the bluff road at a working trot, unhurried, professionals executing the second half of an instruction whose first half had just completed — and the instruction, Cael wrote that night, had never been *take him*. It had been *measure the door*. The order was a probe: whether paper of that grade would move a chartered institution, at no cost if it didn't. The refusal had cost them nothing. The attempt had bought them everything they came for, which was the exact weight of Halcenvane's procedure, tested at the gate, logged, and carried away.
    78	
    79	Withrow stood in her arch until the four of them were off the bluff's shoulder and out of sight. Then she said, to Bracken, in a voice that carried up to the gallery because she had not troubled to keep it from doing so, "Log the hour. Log the reading. Log the offer to the enrollee, verbatim, and note that it was made upward into an empty gallery." And she went in.
    80	
    81	The yard was empty inside two minutes and the academy was not.
    82	
    83	Halcenvane held four hundred and thirty enrollees and the news reached all of them by the midday bell, in the way news moved in a walled place: through the refectory queue, corrected twice on the way. By the first telling the riders had been six. By the second they had carried a warrant. By the third — which was the one Cael overheard, standing in the queue in front of two second-years who did not know what he looked like from behind — the chancellor had refused an order from Ostrand and the academy was going to be closed for it.
    84	
    85	He did not correct them. He got his food and sat down and catalogued the room instead, because a room was a document and this one had been rewritten between breakfast and noon.
    86	
    87	Four hundred and thirty enrollees. Thirty-one tables. Two of the thirty-one had a space at them that had not been there the day before — the delegation's, and the third-years' by the west windows, where the cohort ate and where the seat beside Cael's usual place stayed open through the whole of the meal with a full room around it. Not hostility. Nobody said anything to him at all, which was the point: the academy had discovered by noon that being near the respondent was a thing with a price on it, and four hundred-odd people had begun, individually and without malice, to work out what the price was.
    88	
    89	Rooke came and sat in the open seat with his plate, at the west windows, in front of the cohort, and ate a whole meal there and said nine words in the course of it, all of them about hips and one strapped shoulder with three days still to run on its five. Ephram sat down after him. Then two of the third-years, and then the rest of them, and by the meal's end the table was full and the arithmetic of the room had been publicly answered by a coach who had not once mentioned the gate.
    90	
    91	Gault stopped Cael on the covered walk that afternoon, which he had not done in a term, and said one thing, in the panel-note register he never left: "The evaluator of record is required to be informed of any assessment of an enrollee under provision. I was not informed. I have written that down." He inclined his head half an inch and went on to his rooms, and Cael stood on the walk with the driest sentence anyone had said to him all day and found that it had steadied him more than any of the others.
    92	
    93	
    94	---
    95	
    96	He had spent the standoff on the fourth rider.
    97	
    98	Three of the four had surfaces. The lead rider managed his — a professional flatness over a working attention; readable, with patience: he checked the records hall steps twice in twelve minutes, at the two moments the yard's traffic changed, which meant he had rated the steps as the yard's most likely source of a complication and had rated it correctly. The two flankers were escort-grade: competent, bored at the correct depth, their attention on the yard's exits out of habit rather than assignment, one of them shifting his weight every ninety seconds or so in the way of a man whose boots were newer than his job. The instrument had all three inside a minute and found nothing worth a second minute.
    99	
   100	The fourth never spoke, and Cael read her for the whole of the standoff and got, for the second time in his life, almost nothing — and this time the almost had structure.
   101	
   102	A compact, unhurried woman of no particular age, in traveling grey, who had dismounted last. She had led her horse three steps after dismounting and then stopped, and had not moved again, and Cael's eye kept returning to where she stood without at first knowing why.
   103	
   104	He ran the geometry from the gallery, the way he ran floors before bouts. It took him four passes.
   105	
   106	The first pass gave him nothing but a woman standing in a yard. The second gave him the first oddity, which was that she had stopped three steps from where a person naturally stops, and that the three steps had taken her off the cobbles' fall-line and onto the yard's one level patch, which he knew was level because he had crossed it eight hundred times. The third pass gave him the arch: she stood at the angle from which the arch's whole span was open to her, which no one else in the yard had, because the arch was twelve feet deep and its span closed to anything not standing in a particular six-foot band.
   107	
   108	The fourth pass gave him the rest of it, all at once, the way a floor's document assembles itself when the last line lands.
   109	
   110	She stood at the single point commanding every exit the yard had. The gate arch behind her at the angle that gave her its whole span. The lodge door, the records hall steps, the covered walk's stair, the stable lane — every line of movement out of that yard crossed her position or her sightline, and the stable lane, which was the only exit without a door, was the one she had squared her shoulders to. She had found the point on arrival, without surveying, without a circuit, without once turning her head to count the ways out, the way Cael found rails.
   111	
   112	And she was laying nothing.
   113	
   114	That was the almost-nothing's structure: no declaration, no architecture, no working — the Iron-adjacent read, run at her at full depth from the gallery across forty feet, returned a practitioner at rest, and returned it with the specific texture of a practitioner whose rest was itself a discipline. No tells because nothing was being suppressed. No readable stance because the stance had been retired somewhere below visibility.
   115	
   116	He tried three times to get more. He ran the read at the moment the lead rider spoke, when attention shifts and every practitioner in a yard leaks a fraction; she leaked nothing. He ran it at the word *voluntarily*, which was the morning's one moment of live tension; nothing. He ran it at the remount, when a body has to commit to a physical act and the commitment is legible in advance for about a half-second; and she got onto a horse the way a clerk gets onto a stool, and the half-second was not there, and he understood that it had been trained out of her by somebody a long time ago.
   117	
   118	Just placement, held, for the length of the standoff, at the one point that mattered, by a person whose whole craft appeared to be position and whose position was a sentence.
   119	
   120	The observation notebook got its entry that night, and the entry opened a page the notebook had never had:
   121	
   122	*Fourth rider, unnamed. Path unknown — nothing shown. Working hypothesis from inference alone: containment craft. Fixed-point thinking — she holds position the way Shield holds coverage, but aimed outward, at the space, not the self. Every exit priced. Nothing projected; everything anchored. If Shield is a wall around a person, this is a cage around a yard, built out of nothing but where she stands and what she could do from there.*
   123	
   124	*Test of the hypothesis, since a hypothesis I can't test is a guess with better handwriting: if she is containment, her position should be indifferent to the conversation and sensitive only to the geometry. Check — she did not shift once in twelve minutes, through a reading, a refusal, and an offer, and the only thing that would have moved her is somebody walking. Second test: containment doesn't need to see faces, only lines. Check — she never looked at Withrow. Not once. The chancellor of the institution was speaking twenty feet in front of her and the woman was watching the stable lane.*
   125	
   126	*I have seen discipline of this grade exactly twice. Seln, who is absent from every sightline that matters. Vastin, who is present in all of them and shows nothing. She is a third kind: present, visible, unhidden — and unreadable because the whole practitioner has been folded down into placement.*
   127	
   128	*What I could not do: get any read at all on what she would have done if somebody had walked. That's the gap in the page and I'm marking it, because a gap you've marked is cheaper than a gap you've forgotten. I don't know her range. I don't know her rate. I don't know what it costs her, and everything costs somebody something. I watched a practitioner work for twelve minutes and came away with a shape and no numbers.*
   129	
   130	*Query for Seln: what Path holds people?*
   131	
   132	---
   133	
   134	Seln answered the query in the records hall that evening, with the escort order's copy — Bracken had made one before returning the original; registrars copied — flat on the hall's long table between the inner circle, and he answered it in one sentence, and added the fact that mattered in a second, and the two sentences rearranged the term.
   135	
   136	"Anchor Path," he said. "Containment architecture — anchored force lattices, perimeter binding, suppression laid as fixed points. Rare. Unglamorous. It is the Path the system uses to hold people, and for the obvious reason it is disproportionately represented in Compact enforcement service." He turned his cup a quarter-turn. "Anchor specialists at that grade work enforcement. Enforcement doesn't do assessment escorts. Whoever wrote that paper is done assessing."
   137	
   138	"How rare," said Cael.
   139	
   140	"One in four hundred at Kindling. Perhaps fewer; the numbers are poorly kept because nobody has ever wanted them." Seln's eyes were on the copy. "It is not a Path a person chooses and it is not a Path anyone celebrates. There are no Anchor champions. There is no Anchor bracket, because two Anchor practitioners on a floor together produce nothing a gallery would pay for. What there is, is service — perimeter work, transport, custody, containment of practitioners whose Paths make ordinary custody meaningless. The service takes nearly all of them within two years of Kindling and trains them the way you saw, and the way you saw is the whole doctrine: a fixed point costs almost nothing to hold and everything to break, so the craft is to stand in the right place and never need to."
   141	
   142	"Your read on her grade," said Karis, without looking up.
   143	
   144	"Twelve minutes without a shift, in a yard with a live refusal in it, is not a grade you get from four years of service." He set the cup down. "That is somebody's principal instrument."
   145	
   146	The table sat with the two sentences and with what he had built on them.
   147	
   148	Karis had the order's copy in front of her and was reading the seal's impression with a glass — she had borrowed Bracken's; the registrar owned three — and she said, without looking up, "The seal's format is registry. The office is unverifiable. Bracken's query to the district seat will come back in a week and it will say the office was constituted recently, correctly, by an instrument signed under a seal that is also a format and also unverifiable, and the trail will be paper the whole way down and there will be no person in it anywhere." She set the glass down. "The faceless layer has learned to write paper. That's what today was. They tested whether their paper works on us. It doesn't — yet. So they've learned that too."
   149	
   150	"Say the *yet*," said Brom.
   151	
   152	"Today the paper failed because Bracken could not place the seal and Withrow chose to make the failure the whole issue," Karis said. "Both of those are people. Put that same order in front of a gate lodge with no Bracken behind it, or a chancellor who would rather not be a named respondent for a second term, and it works. It is a good instrument. It failed at the one gate on the continent with two people at it who have made a career of reading paper, and I would like everyone to notice how thin that is."
   153	
   154	"What does the layer do," Lira said, "when the paper doesn't work?"
   155	
   156	Nobody answered her.
   157	
   158	But the table's silence had a direction, and the direction was the man at the bench's middle, who had not spoken since his answer about the woman's grade, and who was looking at the escort order's copy — had been looking at it, Cael realized, charting backward, since it was laid on the table; through Karis's seal-reading, through the question about the *yet*, through Lira's question and the silence after it — longer than anyone, with the stillness the delegation had learned to read as the man's equivalent of a raised voice.
   159	
   160	Cael watched him do it and did not interrupt, and charted what there was to chart, which was almost nothing and was the most he had ever got: that Seln had not touched the copy; that his hands were flat on the table on either side of it, which they were not, ever; that at some point in the last quarter hour he had stopped turning his cup.
   161	
   162	The log, that night, closed on him.
   163	
   164	*A man looking at a piece of genuine registry stock from an office that does not exist, in a hand he has not commented on, with a face that has stopped being furniture. The rest of us are reading the paper. He's reading something behind it, and whatever it is, he's been reading it for fifteen years, and tonight it moved.*
   165	
   166	*Vastin said the people who want this to stop wouldn't use official channels much longer. Today the unofficial channel knocked at the gate, politely, in genuine paper. It'll knock once more somewhere without a Withrow in front of it. Whatever we're going to do, the schedule isn't ours anymore.*
     1	# Chapter 27 — Measure the Door
     2	
     3	The lodge-man wrote the hour on the back of his hand.
     4	
     5	He did it with the stub of pencil from behind his ear, standing in his own doorway, while the last of the four horses was still going down the bluff road below the gate, because his book was on the counter behind him and he did not want to lose the minute in the turning round. Cael saw him do it from the wall stair, coming down. Then the old man went in, and took the book off the counter, and copied the figure off his hand into it, and licked his thumb and rubbed the hand clean.
     6	
     7	By the time Cael reached the lodge door, Bracken was at the counter beside him with his senior clerk, and the house's book was open between them, and Bracken was giving the morning to the book in the order the book would want it.
     8	
     9	Withrow had told him how, before she went in. Cael had heard her from the gatehouse. *All of it in the book, Registrar.* Now the registrar stood with his spectacles on the end of his nose and dictated, flat and even. The senior clerk wrote it in the hand he kept for the enrollment book, and Cael stood in the lodge door and listened to the morning come back to him one ruled line at a time.
    10	
    11	*Arrived at the second bell. Four, mounted, in travelling grey, no livery. Departed at a quarter before the third.*
    12	
    13	*An order presented, under a seal of the registry's pattern, naming an issuing office unknown to this counter. Read aloud in the court at the Chancellor's request, in full, by the lead rider.*
    14	
    15	He had sat behind the bell-frame for that. He had counted it as it came back off the walls. Eighty-one words, and his registry name at the top of them.
    16	
    17	*The Chancellor declined the order pending verification, on four grounds, as follows.*
    18	
    19	Bracken did not give them in her order. He gave them in the order a registrar would want to find them in a hurry, with the person first, and the clerk numbered them down the margin as they came.
    20	
    21	*One. The young man named is enrolled here in good standing and is a party before the seat, and he is produced where the code says and when, and not otherwise.*
    22	
    23	*Two. This counter cannot place the office that sealed the order.*
    24	
    25	*Three. Nothing served on this house since the eighth day of the year speaks of moving him anywhere.*
    26	
    27	*Four. The seat's own schedule has no assessment in it.*
    28	
    29	Cael had heard her give them the other way round, from the schedule up to the young man, so that the last of them landed on him. On the page they began with him. He thought both orders were right, for the different rooms they were said in.
    30	
    31	"Next," said Bracken, and stopped, and took his spectacles off.
    32	
    33	The clerk waited with the pen lifted.
    34	
    35	"*An offer was made by the lead rider to the enrollee,*" said Bracken. "*In these words.*" He gave them exactly. Cael could have given them himself. "*The order is addressed to two parties. The second may come with us of his own accord. If he comes voluntarily, nobody here need refuse anybody anything, and we can all be gone before the third bell.*"
    36	
    37	The pen went along the line.
    38	
    39	"*The offer was spoken upward, toward the gatehouse roof,*" said Bracken. "*There was nobody on the gatehouse roof.*"
    40	
    41	The clerk wrote that too. He did not look at Cael in the doorway, and neither did Bracken.
    42	
    43	Gault was in the lodge doorway beside Cael. Nobody had seen him come across the court; he had his case clamped under one arm and a small black notebook in his other hand, and he had evidently been standing there for the last two entries.
    44	
    45	"Registrar," he said. "If you would add that the evaluator of record was not told of any assessment proposed for the enrollee. Before it was proposed, or since."
    46	
    47	Bracken gave it to the clerk in his flat voice, and the clerk wrote it. Gault opened his own notebook on his forearm and wrote in it too, standing in the doorway, a short line, and closed it on the pencil.
    48	
    49	"I have written that down," said Gault, to nobody in particular, and went away across the court toward the Magisters' stair.
    50	
    51	Withrow's eyes had stayed on the rider the whole morning. "You are speaking to me, Officer," she had said. "There is nobody else in this court you have any business speaking to. If your office wants something from a student of mine, it writes to me." That was not going in the book. Bracken did not dictate it. It had not been addressed to the record.
    52	
    53	The last entry Bracken gave the clerk was the shortest. *No refusal in writing was requested.* The clerk wrote it and sanded the page.
    54	
    55	Withrow had not gone straight in. Cael saw her stop at the lodge door on her way across the court, where the lodge-man stood with the book under his arm and the hour still faintly grey on the back of his hand, and look up once more at the board he had painted.
    56	
    57	"*Anything sealed, fetch the registrar,*" she read aloud. "You did, and you shut your window, and you stood where you could see the whole of it." She nodded to him, the nod she gave her senior staff. "Your board did very well this morning. Put a fresh coat on it in the spring, if you would. I should like it to last."
    58	
    59	The lodge-man said he would, Chancellor, and stood straighter against his jamb than Cael had ever seen him stand, and did not sit down again on his high stool until she had gone round the corner of the lecture range.
    60	
    61	There was nothing in the house's book about the woman.
    62	
    63	There was no line on the form for her and no reason Bracken should have thought of one. She had said nothing and done nothing and handed nobody anything. Cael stood in the lodge door while the clerk sanded the page, and gave her the lines himself, in his head, because nobody else was going to.
    64	
    65	He had read her four times. The first two he already had: three paces in, onto the one level patch in the court. The third time he had asked the court what she could see from there, and the answer had been the arch. The gatehouse was twelve feet deep. From almost anywhere in the court you saw only a slice of the road through it, the left side from the covered walk, the right from the lecture range. There were a few feet of flag from which the whole opening lay open end to end, road and verges and the first bend below the gate. She had stood in them with her back three-quarters to the arch, and never once turned to look.
    66	
    67	The fourth time he had drawn lines. One to the arch behind her; one to the lodge door; one into the corner where the covered walk ran through to the quadrangle; one round the end of the lecture range; one into the stable passage, which had never had a door on it and never would. Every line went past her. Three went within a stride of her boots. And her shoulders had been squared, a hand's breadth, to the last of them: the one way out of that court the house itself could not have closed.
    68	
    69	And all that while she had laid nothing. He had run the read at her at full depth across forty feet of stone, at the moment the word went up, when anyone standing in a court with a refusal going on in it lets go of a fraction of something. She let go of nothing. It was not hiding; hiding has a grain to it, and he had learned that grain from two years of Seln at a counter. There was simply nothing there. She was a person standing in a court, in exactly the right place.
    70	
    71	An hour after the riders had gone he went up to his sill and took out the observation notebook, and went past two years of the Crown yard, eleven Paths and every fighter on the hill, to the first clean leaf at the back. He wrote a heading the notebook had never needed before.
    72	
    73	*The fourth rider. A woman. Unnamed. Path: not shown.*
    74	
    75	He did not write down the four times he had read her. He had them already, and the page was for what they meant.
    76	
    77	*Guess: the whole craft is where she stands. A Shield guards a person. This guards ground, and whatever has to cross it. If she can do anything from where she stands. I never saw her do anything.*
    78	
    79	*Not known: how far she reaches. How fast. What it costs her. Nothing is free, so it costs her something, and I can't see what. A shape and no numbers.*
    80	
    81	He looked at that for a while. Then he wrote one more line at the bottom, by itself, because there was only one person on the hill who might know.
    82	
    83	*Query for Seln: what Path holds people?*
    84	
    85	---
    86	
    87	At noon he took the guess into the refectory and found it waiting for him.
    88	
    89	The third-years' long table under the west windows was full from end to end, as it was every noon, elbows touching. There was one place empty at it, and it was the place on Cael's left. Somebody had been going to sit there. Somebody had come to it with a tray, and looked at who was sitting next to it, and found a place somewhere else.
    90	
    91	He sat down and looked at the empty place the way he had looked at the court that morning. One spot in a full room where nobody would stand. Every line in the room went round it.
    92	
    93	Rooke sat down in it.
    94	
    95	He came with a full plate, and he did not look at Cael, and he did not say anything about the gate. He put his elbows on the table and began to eat, steadily, and between mouthfuls he talked across the board to the Current girl about hips. Her hips, and Lira's, and the whole program's. He told her the inside of a landing hip was the first thing to go on a planked floor and the last thing anybody thought to strap, and that Lira's had come back quiet after two days and she was to make it three before she believed it.
    96	
    97	Behind Cael two first-years at the bread were telling each other that there had been ten riders, with swords. Further along, a boy who had heard it from the urn said the lodge-man had been struck and his window broken. The two second-years nearest had it best and worst: the Chancellor had sent Ostrand away from her gate, and Ostrand would shut the house for it, and one of them had a cousin who knew the date.
    98	
    99	Rooke talked about hips through all of it. He talked about hips for a whole meal, to a table of third-years who had never once heard him say more than a sentence about anything, and the rumours went past him on both sides like water past a post.
   100	
   101	Ephram put his tray down on Cael's right, halfway through. Then two of the cohort moved up from the far end. Then the rest of them shifted along, without anybody saying a word, until the table was the table again with no gap anywhere in it. "Grievances are Third-day evenings," Ephram said to nobody in particular, reaching for the bread. "I'm not taking any at lunch."
   102	
   103	Rooke wiped his plate with his bread and took it away. He had not mentioned the gate once. He had stood in the one place in the room nobody else would stand, and the room had moved round him.
   104	
   105	Karis had Bracken's middle glass and the clerk's pencil rubbing of the seal on the long table in the records hall, with the fair copy of the order beside them, when Cael came in at dusk. The others were already there. Seln was at the middle of the bench across from the copy, with his hands folded and his cup at his elbow.
   106	
   107	Karis did not look up.
   108	
   109	"Before anybody says anything about her," she said, "I want the words straight, because they're going to blur if we're not careful, and I'll be the one keeping the notes." She held up one finger. "Ternhall's anchors are mine. Three footholds I plant in a floor, in their order, so that the heat of a contact runs down into the boards and not back up my arm. Ternhall calls the whole frame a lattice." A second finger. "Which has nothing to do with the Lattice Path. That's lines you can't see, strung across a space, that you find by walking into them." She lowered the hand. "And then there's whatever stood in our court this morning. It wasn't either of those. It didn't plant anything and it didn't string anything. I haven't got a word for it."
   110	
   111	"Anchor Path," said Seln.
   112	
   113	They looked at him. He had not opened his eyes.
   114	
   115	"You won't find it on a meet card," he said. "You find it on a posting roll. I've filed from three towns where one of them was billeted, and I never once saw one on a floor. It's rare at any station. The service writes to the family in the week the station enters it, and most of them go, because nobody else has ever written to them about anything. It holds people. That's what the system keeps it for."
   116	
   117	"Holds them how?" said Brom.
   118	
   119	"I've never stood near enough to one at work to say, and I won't guess." Seln opened his eyes and looked at the copy. "I know where they're posted, and it isn't escort. Escort is for clerks on good horses. One at that grade, standing where she stood, is enforcement." He laid one fingertip on the table beside the order, not touching it. "Whoever wrote that paper is done assessing."
   120	
   121	Seln turned his head toward Cael without opening his eyes.
   122	
   123	"Her horse," he said. "When she mounted. Did it shift?"
   124	
   125	"No."
   126	
   127	"Then she's the best they had to spare this morning." Seln faced the copy again. "Nobody keeps a horse like that for escort work."
   128	
   129	Cael wrote *Anchor Path* in the notebook under the morning's page, and under it Karis's three words in her order, and ruled a line beneath them.
   130	
   131	Bracken came in at the hall's side door with a sheet in his hand and his pen behind his ear. He had been at his counter since the riders left. He did not sit; he never sat in that room when there were enrollees in it.
   132	
   133	"The letter to the district seat," he said, and read it to them; he read aloud everything the house was going to send. The date; the instrument as presented, quoted whole; the issuing office as named; *the registrar of this house is unable to place the said office in any register available to him, and respectfully asks that the instrument constituting it be supplied, together with the seal under which that instrument was itself issued.* He lowered the sheet. "It goes down with the morning bag. They will answer inside the week, because they must."
   134	
   135	Karis opened her notebook to the date a week ahead and wrote *DISTRICT REPLY*. On the next leaf she wrote *REPLY* again and left the office blank. Brom told her she was scheduling paper she had not seen. She put an empty bracket round the following three leaves. "I'm scheduling the absence of a name," she said.
   136	
   137	Bracken looked at the bracket. "Leave me one more leaf after those." He folded the letter once. "If the replies never give us a person, the book will at least show how many times we asked for one." He looked round the table, a little over his spectacles. "It is not much. It is what a true book is for."
   138	
   139	He went back out to his counter.
   140	
   141	Karis turned the rubbing over and looked at the grey circle of the seal for a while, the registry's pattern standing up white out of the pencil.
   142	
   143	"It's a good die," she said. "Somebody cut it properly." She put her palm flat on it. "The faceless layer has learned to write paper."
   144	
   145	She did not go on. She looked at the side door Bracken had gone out by, and then up, at the ceiling, toward the Chancellor's rooms on the floor above, and Cael watched her count something on the two of them and not say what it came to.
   146	
   147	It was Brom who stood up.
   148	
   149	"Show me," he said, to Cael. "Where she stood."
   150	
   151	---
   152	
   153	Brom was already standing in the chalk ring when Cael got up off his knees.
   154	
   155	They had come out to the Crown yard with two lanterns and a lump of the porter's chalk. At the yard gate Cael had looked back and found that Seln had come too, unasked, a few steps behind them, with his coat buttoned to the chin and his hands in its pockets. Seln had not come onto the oak. He stood at its edge by the porter's boards, where the year's names stood chalked pale in the lamplight, Ephram's at the head of the Iron column, and watched.
   156	
   157	Cael had drawn the outer court at its own size, fifty feet by seventy, paced once by him and once by Brom until they agreed. The arch as two long lines, twelve feet apart. The lodge door in its cheek. The covered walk's corner. The mouth of the lane. The stable passage, with no door drawn across it. In the middle, a square for the capped well and a ring round it for the level flags; and Brom, before the chalk was out of Cael's hand, had walked into the ring three paces from the arch and turned his shoulders a hand's breadth toward the stable passage.
   158	
   159	"Here?"
   160	
   161	"A little left. There."
   162	
   163	Brom moved, and stood. "Now walk out."
   164	
   165	Cael walked out. Middle to the arch, and back; middle to the lodge door; to the covered walk's corner; to the lane; to the stable passage; chalking each road on the oak behind him as he went, the way he chalked a fighter's footwork before a bout. Five lines out of one court.
   166	
   167	Every one went past Brom. Three went within a stride of him. One went straight through the place he stood, and he had to step aside to let Cael by. The line to the stable passage started almost under his boots.
   168	
   169	"Try it," said Brom. "Get out past me. The doorless way."
   170	
   171	Cael went from the lodge door's mark toward the stable passage at a walk, and Brom took one step, only one, sideways off the ring, and was standing in the line. Cael went the long way round, by the covered walk's corner, and Brom took one step back, and was in that line too. He had not hurried. He had hardly moved his feet.
   172	
   173	"One step," said Brom. "From there it's one step to anywhere you want to go." He looked down at the chalk. "And that's me. I can't hold anything. I'm just a big man standing in the right place."
   174	
   175	The long wall threw their voices back late and thin, so that everything said on the oak was said twice, the way the rider's voice had come back off the court that morning. Without agreeing to, they had all three begun to speak low.
   176	
   177	"They didn't want you this morning," said Brom, looking down at his feet on the drawn flags. "Not really. Four of them, and one of those, and they turned round at the first no."
   178	
   179	"They came to measure the door," said Cael.
   180	
   181	Brom considered the chalk arch, twelve feet deep, and the open stable passage. "Well," he said. "Now they've got the figure." Then, after a while: "I'd stand there too, if I was holding it. I'd never have found it in three steps."
   182	
   183	"What would she do from there?" said Cael. "If somebody walked."
   184	
   185	"I don't know."
   186	
   187	"Nor do I." Cael looked at the five chalk lines running out from under Brom across the oak. "I don't know how far she reaches. I don't know that she reaches at all. I've drawn where she stood and where everybody else would have to go. It's a floor with nothing on it about the fighter."
   188	
   189	"Then that's what it is," said Seln, from the edge.
   190	
   191	They both looked round.
   192	
   193	"Count what you've got. Five lines and a place to stand. Write those down." He took one hand out of his pocket and moved it a little, at the bare oak round the chalk. "Leave the rest blank, where you'll see it every time you open the page. A blank you can see is worth something. It's the blanks you've forgotten that get people hurt."
   194	
   195	Brom stayed where he was a while longer, on the drawn flags with the lanterns at his feet. Then he stepped out of the ring carefully, the way a man steps out of a place he means to remember. They left the chalk for the porter to wash off in the morning, and picked up the lanterns, and went back.
   196	
   197	The cup had not moved.
   198	
   199	That was what Cael saw first when they came back into the records hall: Seln's cup, at his elbow on the long table, exactly where it had stood when they went out. Karis and Lira were still at the table, Karis copying the evening into her notebook, Lira with her landing leg along the bench and her eyes shut. Seln sat down at the middle of the bench where he had sat before, and did not touch it.
   200	
   201	Seln turned his cups. He always had. A quarter-turn when he sat down, a quarter-turn back when he had said what he meant to say, a quarter-turn on the counter when he had finished a cup of the lodge-man's tea. Cael had watched him do it for two years without ever once remarking on it. Tonight the cup had stood at his elbow for a whole evening, through Anchor Path and the rubbing and Bracken's letter, and he had not turned it once.
   202	
   203	Cael thought, at first, that the man was tired. It had been a long day for everyone, and Seln was older than any of them and had been up before the bell to do the post-room sort. Cael sat down across from him and put the lantern on the table and said the thing that had been in his head since the oak.
   204	
   205	"Is there any way to find out how far she reaches? Without standing in front of her?"
   206	
   207	Seln did not answer.
   208	
   209	He was not tired. Cael saw that as soon as he had asked. Seln's two hands were on the table, flat, one on each side of the fair copy of the order, close to the paper but not touching it, and his eyes were on the paper, and they had been on it, Cael understood now, since the moment he sat down.
   210	
   211	Karis finished her copying and set down her pen. Lira opened her eyes. Brom sat with his elbows on his knees. And Seln went on looking at the order, longer than any of them, with his hands flat and still on either side of it.
   212	
   213	"Fifty-one minutes," said Brom, to the floor between his boots. "That clock room at Norhold. And you came out of it with one sentence for us."
   214	
   215	He did not say which sentence. He did not need to. Every one of them had carried it home from Norhold and up this hill, and Cael heard it again now in the Archmarshal's dry voice, among forty clocks: *Because the people who want this to stop are not going to use official channels much longer.* The sentence no longer ended in the clock room. In Cael's mind it went on down the bluff road, past the arch Withrow had held that morning, toward doors where no chancellor would be waiting.
   216	
   217	Seln moved.
   218	
   219	He took his right hand off the table and reached for his cup, and Cael thought he was going to turn it at last. He did not. He picked it up and set it down again a full hand's breadth further away from the order, on the far side of his own reach, as if it had been standing too near something; and he put the hand back flat beside the paper, where it had been.
   220	
   221	Cael said it aloud, because it was true, and because somebody at that table had to.
   222	
   223	"Whatever we're going to do, the schedule isn't ours anymore."
   224	
   225	Nobody answered him. And Seln, at the middle of the bench, with his hands flat on either side of the paper, went on looking at it.

