editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-59.md:3:Vastin went down the tower stair at his ordinary pace and out under the arch.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-59.md:5:There was a big young man sitting on the step there with bandaged forearms, watching the court. He looked up as Vastin passed. Vastin knew him from the row: the continental Copper champion, the bluff's wall. He nodded to him, as one nods to a man keeping a door. The young man nodded back, gravely, and went on watching the court.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-59.md:69:"Then grade it both ways," she said, "and keep both. As a report it's poor. As a man he's good. One doesn't cancel the other. What it means is that the warning is true and we can't prove it, both at once." She looked at the page. "That's the worst kind of warning there is. It's also the only kind anybody ever gets in time to use."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-59.md:73:"Wardens threaten. Politicians hint. That was neither." The pouch's strap went through its buckle under his fingers. "That was an honest man spending career capital to tell you the truth a few months early. Treat the warning as real, because it is."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-59.md:85:"Fifty-one minutes," she said, so that only he heard. "That's what mine took. At the station, with the Arbiter." She put the pot down. "Make of that what you like."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-59.md:115:*First honest warning I've ever gotten from inside the machine. He built no case, made no ask, took no note. He just told me the weather. The Compact's best evaluator thinks the next thing that comes for me won't wear a uniform or file a challenge. Write it down. Believe it. Keep competing anyway — but from tonight, we build like people who believe it.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-58.md:65:*The Archmarshal Vastin asks the enrollee for one hour, before the houses leave the city. Nothing will be written by either party. It would be better if nothing were brought to write with. The timekeepers' office in the east tower has lent a room for the purpose; it belongs to neither the adjudication office nor the Compact. He is free to say no; it will count against him nowhere, with no one, at any time; and that sentence is not a formality.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-58.md:139:Vastin was sitting at the long table with his back to the window. He got up when Cael came in.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-58.md:143:His eyes went over Cael once. They did it as they had on the bluff, without any hurry and without seeming to do it at all. They rested a moment where the strapping showed under the shirt, and a moment on the wrapped forearm, and on the care with which Cael lowered himself into the chair across the table. Vastin said nothing about any of it. Cael saw him take each thing and put it away, and understood that this was a kind of good manners. In his trade you did not comment on what you found. You kept it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-58.md:145:"Thank you for coming," said Vastin, and sat.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-58.md:153:Vastin looked round the room before he did, at the shelves of clocks.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-58.md:163:"I'm going to give you eight words," said Vastin. "Four, and then four. I have watched them come across my table this year in letters about your file, the first four in the early part of it, the second four since. I want you to hear them in the order they came." He said the first four slowly, each one by itself.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-58.md:173:"The first four are words you use about a fault," said Vastin. "A thing gone wrong in the record, which a careful office would like to find and mend. You find a fault so that you can take it out." He moved his left hand an inch on the wood. "The second four are words you use about a thing you mean to keep in a box. I went through the Compact's charter for them, article by article, one evening. They aren't in it. You can't write *handling* about a question. You can only write it about an answer you've already got."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-58.md:183:Then Vastin lifted his eyes to Cael's and kept them there.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-58.md:189:"Because the people who want this to stop are not going to use official channels much longer."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-58.md:209:Vastin did not answer.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-58.md:213:"I won't answer that one," said Vastin.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-58.md:215:That was all. But it was a great deal. He had not said *I don't know*, which would have been true and easy. He had looked for whoever set that line in the file, as Ilsev had, and found nobody; and it troubled him. It troubled him more than anything else in the hour, more than the eight words, more than the warning. He had just said a thing in this room that could end forty years if anybody heard it, and he would not say this. Cael understood that it was not the record he was wary of. It was the blank in it. A man who has spent his life learning exactly how each instrument is wrong does not like an error that belongs to no instrument at all.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-58.md:219:"No." Vastin did not soften it. "That is the other half of why I asked for you. What you do now makes no difference to it. A year ago it would have. A thing you do is an answer, and answers are for questions, and they have stopped asking. Nothing answers a conclusion."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-58.md:227:Vastin did not ask what he meant. He looked, for a moment, very slightly pleased.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-58.md:243:He did not offer protection. He said, in so many words, that he had none to give, because it would have been dishonest to let the boy wonder. Whatever reach his office had, it had through the very channels he had just told Cael would be stepped round. And a warning from a man who could also shelter you, he said, would have been a smaller thing than this, because you could never be sure which of the two he was giving you.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-58.md:271:"Fifty-one minutes."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/STATE_LEDGER.md:14:| Lira | Wind Path, Iron-tier, Rank One, formal: the Ostrand registry station's Arbiter evaluation on the fifth of the new month, fifty-one minutes ("They didn't give me anything today. They caught up."); the porter changed the word and moved her name the next day; Gault filed, Ilsev witnessing. Season record certified "Eleven bouts unbeaten. None lost." The Copper Crown is retired on the shelf over the settle ("There. It's retired."). Fit: the semifinal's hip strain (deep, nothing torn) had a month's knitting left at term's end, and nothing of it carries into this book. She trains at dawn on Rooke's speed line. She meant to write to Fiske over the recess (Fiske: "North tier, next season. I'll be watching the Iron bracket."); whether she did is open to M1–M3. [B4-reconciled 2026-10-05] |
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/STATE_LEDGER.md:20:| Vastin | The Archmarshal Vastin (his B4 title throughout, ch38–59). Last seen in B4 at the posting-house at the turn of the registry road on the evening of the twenty-fourth of Reaping, writing the file note and a four-sentence private letter to Bracken. His desk in the Compact's western administration (third floor, river side) is the source's, not B4's, and stands only until the text says otherwise. **No stated age** (§12 C2; OWNER #8, #17); career "forty years". His left hand "had begun to dislike evenings as well as mornings". [B4-reconciled 2026-10-05] |
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/STATE_LEDGER.md:24:**Cael knows:** his six fragments, with their costs and deployment rules (the ch62 inventory: "Six and an anomaly. It isn't a seventh anything."); that the mechanism is integration of witnessed abilities under the stakes conditions; the line the four signed at Halcenvane the year before, the minute of three numbered rules (conditions not manufactured; a directed acquisition only within earnest engagement the other party begins, awake and directed; full cost written beforehand), for which *directed, open-eyed, or not at all* is this book's shorthand and never B4's wording; that Seln has functionally switched (the corridor on the twenty-seventh; the unchanged rotations; the recess counter sheet) and that "Gratitude is the leak"; that one unsigned slip of wing paper came to him inside a returned coursework folder on the night of the Copper final, learned and burned on the common-room coals, and that he and Karis alone know it; that Vastin wrote a note he believes says he is not an error (inferred, not seen; OWNER #10; "in a hand I'd wager signs its whole name"); that the Level 4 designation exists and nobody can trace it; that the fielding clause exists, with its text verbatim (sixth part, subsection four); the year's finding, "The system isn't confused about me"; and the tournament's three-year cycle and Norhold as this cycle's host. He does **not** know the Standard's mechanics yet (that comes in the map room); the exhibition provision's wording; the squad clause; Daeva beyond rumor; what Seln's reports said; or anything about the office above Vastin. [B4-reconciled 2026-10-05]
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/STATE_LEDGER.md:26:**Only the circle (Lira, Brom, Karis) knows the mechanism.** Seln does not, and nor do Ephram, Rooke, Withrow, Bracken, Gault, Hesk or Vastin. Rooke suspects a gap between Cael's record and his ceiling and has chosen never to ask.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/STATE_LEDGER.md:32:**Vastin knows:** his own evaluation and file note (*Classification error is unlikely. Continue observation.*, signed with his whole name; the handsome sentence struck at the close and the amendment written that night); that the designation on the enrollee's sheet was entered by the Kindling in the file's first minutes and that nobody at any height has since amended or questioned it ("So the record was not broken. It was doing something.", which he did not write); the wing's six filings, read once and put "a good way under the record"; nothing of fragments; nothing of Seln's null reports. Whether he knows the Level 4 watch by name is not on B4's page (BOOK_MAP §5 allows it in his cutaways). [B4-reconciled 2026-10-05]
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/STATE_LEDGER.md:41:- **Documents:** three copies and a certified transcription of the tournament charter (Karis read it on the afternoon of Withrow's address; Withrow: posted before selection); Bracken's certified season sheets (about three hundred, across the recess); Karis's grey notebook (the clause and *Interesting.*; Ember's three words) and her marbled watcher log; Rooke's profiles table (forty-one names); Seln's cipher page in the small case (the quarterly is gone down the fish steps); Vastin's file note in the Compact's folder (nobody on the bluff has seen it) and his four-sentence private letter to Bracken praising the enrollment index (sent by the morning post; whether Bracken has shown it to anyone is open); and, from Movement 2, the Standard's public documentation (forty pages) and the examiner's annotated manual. [B4-reconciled 2026-10-05]
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/STATE_LEDGER.md:72:| Vastin | One interview (B4 ch56: eleven questions in a twelve-by-fourteen room, the twelfth not asked); civil baseline; nothing warmer or colder yet. On the evaluation morning he looked once at the failing frame and not again at Cael that whole morning. He has decided that if the question ever becomes properly his he will ask it "in a room with the door shut and tell the boy first." [B4-reconciled 2026-10-05] |
