editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-23.md:3:The colors came out of their trunk on the Sixth-day morning, before the sun was over the wall, and smelled of cedar and the inside of a cupboard.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-26.md:95:"None, Advocate. The petition doesn't turn on evidence. It turns on discretion."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-26.md:103:They were from the dairy. He had written them on the way down in the coach that morning, out of habit, to have the argument by him in case anybody in that room should ever ask him for it: Denvash and the brass plate; Vell's ledger; the Greyvane hearing; the grant and two renewals on paper; the inspection; the clause at Norhold; Umber's *no number goes here*. His receipts. He had laid them out a week ago, in order, at full strength, and Karis had called them an induction and he had not had an answer for her.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-26.md:153:Cael saw it from the covered walk, a little after the second bell on the Sixth-day morning, on his way across to the wing. It was a small thing. In two years he had never once seen that window shut in daylight. The lodge-man sat behind it on his high stool from the first bell to the last, with his tea and the bluff road in front of him, and talked to anybody who came up it. Now the shutter came across the glass from the inside, and a moment later the lodge door opened, and the lodge-man stood in it without his hat.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-26.md:199:*In the matter of Caelen Hesk-ward.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-26.md:201:*To the Chancellor of the chartered academy at Halcenvane, and to the enrollee of record therein named. The registry, in support of the proceedings presently convened at the seat at Ostrand, directs the attendance of the said enrollee at the registry facility at the district seat for pre-evaluation assessment, transport and escort provided, effective on presentation. The academy is directed to produce the enrollee to the bearers of this order. Given under the Compact's hand.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-22.md:39:Lira had not got down off the table to say it, or raised her voice. She had said it to her chalk, and gone on writing on the shelf, and that was why Karis had put it in the margin with an *L.* after it. He had stopped. Then he had finished anyway, straight across the table to her: "I'm not leaving the room while the text still works."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-22.md:43:*Prepare both roads. Win the sittings. Keep a box.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-22.md:63:Cael did not need to be told what was under the hand. A square cream envelope with a dark red seal on it, unbroken, which had come up the hill twenty-three days ago and had not been opened since. Under it, the summons, the docket, the district's confirmation with *outcome anticipated* on it in a clerk's round hand, and a sitting on the last Fifth-day of the year at which three assessors and a sigil frame would almost certainly find Brom an Iron. His family's ledger had been waiting for that line since before he could lift a practice weight. In the second box was a grandmother at the head of a long table, and a word he had never once said in the same breath as *east*.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-22.md:67:The cups went round. Lira took hers in both hands and put her boots up on the bench end, and dug her thumbs into the side of her landing hip, which was Rooke's nightly order and which she obeyed with the face of somebody paying a fine. In three days she would be on a strange floor two days north. She blew across her tea before anybody else had spoken.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-22.md:69:"It was the night we asked," she said.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-27.md:83:*Query for Seln: what Path holds people?*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-27.md:143:"It's a good die," she said. "Somebody cut it properly." She put her palm flat on it. "The faceless layer has learned to write paper."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-27.md:223:"Whatever we're going to do, the schedule isn't ours anymore."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-25.md:21:*The respondent institution is directed to produce at the fourth sitting of the seat the complete records of evaluation-linked floor work of the enrollee of record, Caelen Hesk-ward, for the academic year to date, in the form in which they are kept.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-25.md:153:"One of these is the best night of my life," she said.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-21.md:17:On a floor, if you went to the same place three times, the other fighter learned where you liked to go. A carter was no different. Ask one man three questions about the east road and he would remember you in a week, and tell the next man who stood him a cup, and the next man might wear a grey coat. So she had split her question in three, and gone to three people on three days, and asked each of them about one thing that had nothing to do with the other two. Afterward she could add them up herself, on the walk back up the hill, where nobody could see her do the sum.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-21.md:19:The first had been an old man with a mule team, three days before. Rooke really had wanted the new frame cord, and the river barge really had brought it down, and she really had been the only one in the program with a free hour on a Second-day morning. She had carried his manifest for him from the barge to his cart while he complained about the mules, and she had asked him, idly, what all the stamps on it were for.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-21.md:95:Up there, pinned to the east hall door in Rooke's square capitals, was a card with her name on it for the program's first road meet. Rooke had crossed out its date a week ago and chalked another eight days later, when the confluence's stewards wrote that the meet was put back for repairs to their hall; she had minded for an afternoon and then been glad of the eight days. Up there was a red drill-book with a figure in it that was hers, and a broad patient man from the river academy who had written to ask whether he might come and teach her, after she beat him. Up there was a word she had never had before this year, and had to learn to say: *program*. Somebody had built a ladder, and leaned it against a wall, and put her name at the foot of it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-24.md:135:"She's on five," Brom said quietly.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-24.md:221:"Again. Faster. It's there."

