+## Exact line fixes
+
+Each old string below was verified with fixed-string `rg -F -c` to match exactly once in the named file. Fixes 1–4 and 7–10 close the six remaining tracked chains; fixes 5–6 repair the two cut-created continuity seams.
+
+1. `manuscript/chapter-21.md`
+   old: `On a floor, if you went to the same place three times, the other fighter learned where you liked to go. A carter was no different. Ask one man three questions about the east road and he would remember you in a week, and tell the next man who stood him a cup, and the next man might wear a grey coat. So she had split her question in three, and gone to three people on three days, and asked each of them about one thing that had nothing to do with the other two. Afterward she could add them up herself, on the walk back up the hill, where nobody could see her do the sum.`
+   new: `On a floor, the obvious attack taught the other fighter where to wait. The grey coats had made the landing feel like a floor. Lira had learned to let useful talk arrive sideways, then keep her arithmetic for the climb home.`
+
+2. `manuscript/chapter-21.md`
+   old: `"From the day the fourth sitting closes. Before the fortieth day, a respondent who hasn't come back is only absent. On the fortieth, he's fled, and the default goes in." She looked at the figure as if she had never seen one before. "It's a narrow thing. I had it wrong."`
+   new: `"From the day the fourth sitting closes. Before the fortieth day, a respondent who hasn't come back is only absent. On the fortieth, the code changes the entry to *fled*." She looked at the figure as if she had never seen one before. "It's a narrow thing. I had it wrong."`
+
+3. `manuscript/chapter-22.md`
+   old: `Above it, small, squeezed into the margin, was a line he had not watched her write. *S.: eyes open at C.'s last sentence. A long time.* He had not seen that. His own eyes had been on Lira's chalk when he said it. Karis, it seemed, had been looking at Seln.`
+   new: `Above it, small, squeezed into the margin, Karis had written *C. — last sentence* and drawn a line beneath it.`
+
+4. `manuscript/chapter-22.md`
+   old: `"It's the only ground I've ever—" he had said.`
+   new: `He had been halfway through his last example when Lira cut across him.`
+
+5. `manuscript/chapter-26.md`
+   old: `Withrow pitched her voice to four rows.`
+   new: `Withrow had ordered the production answered in the house's own boards.`
+
+6. `manuscript/chapter-27.md`
+   old: `There was no line on the form for her and no reason Bracken should have thought of one. She had said nothing and done nothing and handed nobody anything. Cael stood in the lodge door while the clerk sanded the page, and gave her the lines himself, in his head, because nobody else was going to.`
+   new: `There was no line in the house's book for her and no reason Bracken should have thought of one. She had said nothing and done nothing and handed nobody anything. Cael stood in the lodge door while the clerk sanded the page, and gave her the lines himself, in his head, because nobody else was going to.`
+
+7. `manuscript/chapter-27.md`
+   old: `"They didn't want you this morning," said Brom, looking down at his feet on the drawn flags. "Not really. Four of them, and one of those, and they turned round at the first no."`
+   new: `Brom paced the drawn arch once, heel to toe, then looked back at the ring.`
+
+8. `manuscript/chapter-27.md`
+   old: `Brom considered the chalk arch, twelve feet deep, and the open stable passage. "Well," he said. "Now they've got the figure." Then, after a while: "I'd stand there too, if I was holding it. I'd never have found it in three steps."`
+   new: `Brom considered the chalk arch, twelve feet deep, and the open stable passage. After a while he said, "I'd stand there too, if I was holding it. I'd never have found it in three steps."`
+
+9. `manuscript/chapter-27.md`
+   old: `Karis opened her notebook to the date a week ahead and wrote *DISTRICT REPLY*. On the next leaf she wrote *REPLY* again and left the office blank. Brom told her she was scheduling paper she had not seen. She put an empty bracket round the following three leaves. "I'm scheduling the absence of a name," she said.`
+   new: `Karis opened her notebook near the back and wrote *NO NAME*. Then she turned to the date a week ahead and entered *DISTRICT REPLY*, leaving the intervening leaves blank. Brom told her she was scheduling paper she had not seen. "I'm keeping room for it," she said.`
+
+10. `manuscript/chapter-27.md`
+   old: `Bracken looked at the bracket. "Leave me one more leaf after those." He folded the letter once. "If the replies never give us a person, the book will at least show how many times we asked for one." He looked round the table, a little over his spectacles. "It is not much. It is what a true book is for."`
+   new: `Bracken looked at the blank leaves. "Leave me one more leaf after those." He folded the letter once. "The query should stand beside whatever answer it earns." He looked round the table, a little over his spectacles. "It is not much. It is what a true book is for."`

